["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>The duck you hunt in October is a different bird from the one in December. Know the calendar and you know who is on the water.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 290\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year chart of a BC duck, general pattern 60 to 80 percent. Wintering on open water: mid November to mid March. Courtship and pairing: October to February. Spring migration: March and April. Nesting and broods: April to July. Drake wing moult, flightless 3 to 4 weeks: June to August. Fall migration: August to late November, peaking in October. Your BC duck seasons fall between September and January; see the Rule Book.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"290\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The duck year in BC</text><text x=\"139.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"161.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text><text x=\"183.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"205.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"227.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"249.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"271.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"293.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"315.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text><text x=\"337.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text><text x=\"359.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text><text x=\"381.0\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text><rect x=\"4\" y=\"60\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Winter water</text><rect x=\"359.0\" y=\"64\" width=\"33.0\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"64\" width=\"55.0\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"90\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#eef2e6\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Court and pair</text><rect x=\"326\" y=\"94\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"94\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"120\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spring move</text><rect x=\"172\" y=\"124\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"150\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#eef2e6\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nest, broods</text><rect x=\"194\" y=\"154\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#d9a23a\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"180\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Wing moult</text><rect x=\"238\" y=\"184\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#b9c2cf\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"210\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#eef2e6\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fall move</text><rect x=\"282\" y=\"214\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"4\" y=\"240\" width=\"392\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Duck seasons</text><rect x=\"304\" y=\"244\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"128\" y=\"244\" width=\"22\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">October: peak passage. Late November: freeze up.</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>October: peak fall passage through the Interior <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Late November: freeze up ends most Interior migration <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the year in one paragraph per season</summary><ul><li>Winter: open valley rivers, lakes and Fraser Valley fields and sloughs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Spring: north again from March; plateau lakes open as the ice goes (55 to 60%).</li><li>Summer: nesting on Interior wetlands and potholes, broods, then the wing moult <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Fall: early migrants in August, well under way by late September, peak in October, mostly over by late November (60 to 75%).</li><li>These months are a general pattern from the app migration data. There are no month by month counts for the Thompson or Okanagan (55 to 65%).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Ducks are doing (certainty)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan, Feb</td><td>Wintering on open water; pairs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Mar</td><td>Move north; valley wetlands open first <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">55%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Apr</td><td>Plateau lakes open; arrive to breed <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">55%</span></td></tr><tr><td>May, Jun</td><td>Nesting; broods hatch <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Jul</td><td>Broods grow; drakes moult, flightless <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">55%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Aug</td><td>Early migrants move <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">55%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Sep</td><td>Fall migration under way <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Oct</td><td>Peak passage; plateau lakes start to freeze <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Nov</td><td>Freeze up; birds pack onto valley water <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Dec</td><td>Wintering waters <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: where they winter near you</summary><ul><li>Kamloops: ice free stretches of the South Thompson River (Important Bird Area) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Okanagan: Vaseux Lake (a sanctuary, no hunting) and the north arm of Okanagan Lake <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Fraser Valley: the delta and valley fields and sloughs; Pitt Addington Marsh, Harrison Chehalis and Boundary Bay areas (65 to 90%).</li><li>Fraser delta waterfowl need food from September to April <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>See the Hunt Map layer \"Duck staging and winter waters\" in <a href=\"#/s/mc-ducks-07\">Masterclass 7</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Mallard nesting timeline. Lay about 9 eggs, one a day. Sit about 28 days. Hatch: the ducklings go to water within a day. Grow: they fly at 50 to 60 days. The drake leaves early in incubation, 70 percent. Clutch 5 to 15 eggs; incubation 26 to 30 days, 75 percent. About 3 months from first egg to first flight.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">From first egg to first flight (mallard)</text><rect x=\"6\" y=\"44\" width=\"90\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6d7b0\"/><text x=\"51\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lay</text><text x=\"51\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9 eggs</text><text x=\"51\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1 a day</text><line x1=\"96\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"98.0\" y2=\"98.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"104.0,98.0 93.0,92.0 93.0,104.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"104\" y=\"44\" width=\"90\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9d7b8\"/><text x=\"149\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sit</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">about 28</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">days</text><line x1=\"194\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"196.0\" y2=\"98.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"202.0,98.0 191.0,92.0 191.0,104.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"202\" y=\"44\" width=\"90\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#cfe0ef\"/><text x=\"247\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hatch</text><text x=\"247\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">off to water</text><text x=\"247\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">in a day</text><line x1=\"292\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"294.0\" y2=\"98.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><polygon points=\"300.0,98.0 289.0,92.0 289.0,104.0\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><rect x=\"300\" y=\"44\" width=\"90\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grow</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">fly at 50</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">to 60 days</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"166\" width=\"380\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Drake leaves early in the sit (70%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"208\" width=\"380\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Clutch 5 to 15 eggs; sit 26 to 30 days (75%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"250\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">About 3 months from first egg to flight</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>About 9 eggs, 28 days of sitting <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Ducklings walk to water within a day <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the breeding season in detail</summary><ul><li>Clutch: 5 to 15 eggs, about 9 on average (70 to 75%).</li><li>Incubation: 26 to 30 days, about 28 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Ducklings stay in the nest about 10 hours, then follow the hen to water <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>They fly at about 50 to 60 days <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Breeding in BC: Interior wetlands and potholes, mostly below 500 m for mallards; lesser scaup, canvasback and redhead on plateau lakes at 500 to 1,500 m (85%, BC Breeding Bird Atlas).</li><li>At Tranquille near Kamloops mallards, goldeneyes and wood ducks nest <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>. Tranquille is a no shooting area <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline June to December. June to mid September: drakes in eclipse plumage look like hens. Mid September to mid November: colour comes back. From mid November: full colour; about 90 percent of mallard drakes in a California study were in full colour by mid November, 75 percent. The wing patch stays the same all season, 80 percent. An early season brown drake: read the wing, the bill and the size, not the head.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Drake colour: summer to winter</text><text x=\"45.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jun</text><text x=\"96.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jul</text><text x=\"147.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aug</text><text x=\"198.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sep</text><text x=\"249.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oct</text><text x=\"300.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text><text x=\"351.5\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dec</text><defs><linearGradient id=\"mdk-eclipse-g\" x1=\"0\" x2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#8b5a2b\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#2f6e3b\"/></linearGradient></defs><rect x=\"20\" y=\"72\" width=\"178.5\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"109.25\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Eclipse: looks like a hen</text><rect x=\"198.5\" y=\"72\" width=\"102\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"url(#mdk-eclipse-g)\"/><text x=\"249.5\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Colour back</text><rect x=\"300.5\" y=\"72\" width=\"76.5\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\"/><text x=\"338.75\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Full</text><line x1=\"300.5\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"300.5\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">90% of mallard drakes in colour</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">by mid November (75%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"204\" width=\"380\" height=\"84\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Wing patch: the same all season (80%)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"254\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Early season brown drake? Read the wing,</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"274\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the bill and the size. Not the head.</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Ducks drop all flight feathers at once <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Flightless for about 3 to 4 weeks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: moult facts</summary><ul><li>Unlike most birds, ducks, geese and swans lose all flight feathers together after breeding <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Mallards: about 24 to 26 days flightless in captive birds, about 23 to 24 days in Dutch field work <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Black ducks in Labrador: 27 to 30 days, peak in mid July <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Some drakes fly up to 1,200 km to safe moulting lakes before they moult <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Heavier sea ducks have longer flightless periods <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Drakes then wear the drab eclipse plumage into fall; the colour comes back over October and November (75 to 80%).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 380\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline chart, August to December, of when ducks move south, 75 percent. Blue winged teal: August to early September. Shoveler and pintail: August to early September. Green winged teal: September and October. Mallard, wigeon and gadwall: September and October, staying while water is open into December. Ring necked duck: October. Canvasback and redhead: October and November. Goldeneyes and bufflehead: mid to late October, with the ice. Common merganser: late October to November, one of the last.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"380\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">When they leave (75%)</text>\n  <line x1=\"150.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"150.0\" y2=\"336\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"174.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Aug</text>\n  <line x1=\"198.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"198.0\" y2=\"336\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"222.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text>\n  <line x1=\"246.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"246.0\" y2=\"336\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"270.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text>\n  <line x1=\"294.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"294.0\" y2=\"336\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"318.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text>\n  <line x1=\"342.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"342.0\" y2=\"336\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"366.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dec</text>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"78.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Blue winged teal</text>\n  <rect x=\"150.0\" y=\"60.5\" width=\"64.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"113.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Shoveler, pintail</text>\n  <rect x=\"150.0\" y=\"95.5\" width=\"64.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"148.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Green winged teal</text>\n  <rect x=\"198.0\" y=\"130.5\" width=\"96.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"178.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Mallard, wigeon</text>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"194.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">and gadwall</text>\n  <rect x=\"198.0\" y=\"165.5\" width=\"96.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <rect x=\"294.0\" y=\"165.5\" width=\"96.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#c9b79c\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"218.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Ring necked</text>\n  <rect x=\"246.0\" y=\"200.5\" width=\"48.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"248.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Canvasback</text>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"264.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">and redhead</text>\n  <rect x=\"246.0\" y=\"235.5\" width=\"96.0\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"283.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Goldeneyes</text>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"299.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">and bufflehead</text>\n  <rect x=\"267.7\" y=\"270.5\" width=\"26.3\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"318.5\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Merganser</text>\n  <text x=\"142\" y=\"334.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">common</text>\n  <rect x=\"275.4\" y=\"305.5\" width=\"66.6\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"366\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pale bar: stay while water stays open</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Teal, shoveler, pintail leave first <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Goldeneyes, bufflehead and mergansers go with the ice <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: who goes when</summary><ul><li>Blue winged teal, shoveler and pintail leave breeding areas in August or early September <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Green winged teal: September to October; most are south by November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Mallard, wigeon and gadwall: September or October, and stay as long as water is open <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Ring necked duck in October; canvasback and redhead October to November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Goldeneyes and bufflehead: with the ice, mid to late October, reaching wintering water in November and December <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Common merganser: one of the last, late October to November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Most migration happens at night <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. With a strong tailwind a mallard can cover about 1,300 km (800 miles) in 8 hours, then needs 3 to 7 days to refuel <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five changes in October and November. Drakes are back in colour, 9 in 10 by mid November, 75 percent. Pairs form, courtship peaks October to November, 75 percent. Peak passage through the Interior in October, 60 percent. Freeze up: plateau lakes ice over and birds pack onto low valley water, 65 percent. Divers arrive: goldeneyes come with the ice, 75 percent.\"><rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">What changes in October and November</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"36\" width=\"8\" height=\"46\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Drakes in colour</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">9 in 10 by mid November (75%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"88\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#eef2e6\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"88\" width=\"8\" height=\"46\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pairs form</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"127\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Courtship peaks Oct to Nov (75%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"140\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"140\" width=\"8\" height=\"46\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Peak passage</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"179\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Interior, October (60%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"192\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#eef2e6\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"192\" width=\"8\" height=\"46\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Freeze up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"231\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Plateau lakes ice; birds pack low (65%)</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"244\" width=\"380\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"244\" width=\"8\" height=\"46\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Divers arrive</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"283\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\">Goldeneyes come with the ice (75%)</text></svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Colour back, pairs forming, the big push (60 to 75%).</li><li>Late season: fewer, wiser, fatter birds (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how to hunt the change (opinion)</summary><ul><li>Early October near Kamloops: local mallards, wigeon and green winged teal, plus the first northern birds (Tip, 60%).</li><li>After a cold snap: new birds. Check your ponds again (65%, lore).</li><li>When plateau lakes freeze: hunt valley water, rivers and the bigger lakes that stay open <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Late November: goldeneyes and bufflehead on big lakes; remember the goldeneye limit of 2 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>In District No. 2 (Mission) the season opens 10 October 2026 and runs into January, so you can hunt the wintering birds <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-osoyoos-wetland.jpg\" alt=\"Calm, open water out of the wind holds winter ducks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Calm, open water out of the wind holds winter ducks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: B&amp;B-Traveller, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haynes_Point_Wetland_Project,_Osoyoos_Lake,_BC,_Canada_-_panoramio.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Winter ducks need open water, food and calm <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Clear cold nights cost the most energy <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: winter in detail</summary><ul><li>Body mass usually drops from mid to late winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Ducks roost where it is sheltered; dense cover out of the wind gives a better microclimate <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Snow and ice cover push ducks south once a cold index passes a threshold (80%, Missouri study of mallards).</li><li>Hunter meaning: in a cold snap, find the one open, sheltered patch of water, then ask for permission (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 280\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline chart of the duck seasons by district, September 2026 to January 2027, 99 percent. District 3, Kamloops: 8 September to 23 December. District 8, Okanagan: 23 September to 7 January. District 2, Mission: 10 October 2026 to 24 January 2027. Every district: 8 a day, 24 in possession.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"280\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Duck seasons 2026 to 2027 (99%)</text>\n  <line x1=\"110.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"110.0\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"138.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text>\n  <line x1=\"166.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"166.0\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"194.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text>\n  <line x1=\"222.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"222.0\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"250.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text>\n  <line x1=\"278.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"278.0\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"306.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dec</text>\n  <line x1=\"334.0\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"334.0\" y2=\"236\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"362.0\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86.0\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">District 3</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"102.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Kamloops</text>\n  <rect x=\"123.1\" y=\"73.0\" width=\"196.5\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">District 8</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"162.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Okanagan</text>\n  <rect x=\"151.1\" y=\"133.0\" width=\"195.6\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"206.0\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">District 2</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"222.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Mission</text>\n  <rect x=\"182.3\" y=\"193.0\" width=\"195.1\" height=\"26.0\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8 a day, 24 in possession, every district</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>District 3 opens first; District 2 closes last <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Check the Rule Book before every trip (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: where the exact dates live</summary><ul><li>District No. 3 (Kamloops), No. 8 (Okanagan) and No. 2 (Mission): exact dates and limits are in the Rule Book: <a href=\"#/s/rb-migratory-birds/9\">District No. 2</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-migratory-birds/10\">District No. 3</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rb-migratory-birds/15\">District No. 8</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>No new dates are given here. The Rule Book is the source.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"Teal first, goldeneye last\"</td><td>Migration order <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td></tr><tr><td>9 eggs, 28 days, 50 to 60 to fly</td><td>Nesting (70 to 75%)</td></tr><tr><td>3 to 4 weeks</td><td>Flightless moult <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>\"Ice is the clock\"</td><td>Freeze up moves ducks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td></tr><tr><td>\"October peak, November push\"</td><td>Fall passage (60 to 65%)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Old hand's rule (opinion, widely taught): \"The north wind brings the ducks; the ice sends them on.\"</p></aside><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li>\"Moult in July, no birds fly.\" Drakes are flightless in midsummer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Number trick: 9 eggs plus 28 days plus about 55 days to fly is about 3 months <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Main source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Months, places</td><td>App migration data (Ducks Unlimited, BC)</td></tr><tr><td>Nesting</td><td>Wildlife agency notes</td></tr><tr><td>Moult</td><td>Wildfowl journal studies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source, with date checked</summary><ul><li>App data, data/migration.json, ducks: month bands from <a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/conservation/waterfowl-research-science/field-reports-2024-migration-and-wintering-habitat-update\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited field reports 2024</a> and <a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/conservation/waterfowl-research-science/field-reports-2025-fall-habitat-outlook\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2025</a>, <a href=\"https://pacificbirds.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Conservation-of-Mig-Bird-Habitat-Fraser-River-Delta-Guide-for-Local-Govts-2024.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pacific Birds Habitat Joint Venture</a>, <a href=\"https://www.ibacanada.org/mobile/site.jsp?siteID=BC176\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">South Thompson River Important Bird Area</a>, <a href=\"https://www.ibacanada.org/mobile/site.jsp?siteID=BC262\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Vaseux Lake Important Bird Area</a>. Checked 2026-10-03 (55 to 80%).</li><li><a href=\"https://nwf.org/Educational-Resources/Wildlife-Guide/Birds/Mallard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">National Wildlife Federation, mallard</a>: clutch, incubation. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.pgc.pa.gov:443/Education/WildlifeNotesIndex/Documents/Mallard%20hr.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pennsylvania Game Commission, mallard wildlife note</a> and <a href=\"https://seaandsageaudubon.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/MallardsandDucklings.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sea and Sage Audubon</a>: 28 days, fledge 50 to 60 days. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?lang=en&amp;sp=MALL\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Breeding Bird Atlas</a>: where each duck breeds. Checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Moult: <a href=\"https://wildfowl.wwt.org.uk/index.php/wildfowl/article/download/2770/pdf_249\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wildfowl journal, mallard flightless period</a>, <a href=\"https://www.arlis.org/docs/vol1/A/17591492.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">black duck thesis</a>, <a href=\"https://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.p2kf0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sea duck moult data</a>, <a href=\"https://ducks.ca/stories/conservator/feather-weather\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited Canada, Feather weather</a>. Search preview 2026-10-11 (70 to 85%).</li><li><a href=\"https://www.ducks.ca/assets/2021/10/waterfowl-ID-guide-2020-EN-reader-spreads.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited Canada ID guide</a> and <a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/hunting/waterfowl-id/mallard\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited ID pages</a>: species timing. Checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Migration flight range: <a href=\"https://bt.e-ditionsbyfry.com/article/Marsh+Watch/4739146/817413/article.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited magazine</a> and reposts. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://bioone.org/journals/Journal-of-Wildlife-Management/volume-74/issue-1/2008-524/Weather-Related-Indices-of-AutumnWinter-Dabbling-Duck-Abundance-in-Middle/10.2193/2008-524.full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Schummer and others, weather related indices of dabbling duck abundance, Journal of Wildlife Management (2010)</a>. Search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Seasons: app Rule Book <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul></details>"]